504} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0994d5-5191-5b55-8040-66d25ade9ade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e8d311f-d7c5-51d9-a817-053106442c46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551492} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e16594c-6aa5-5439-b628-092748d91c88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b4a3da3a-1936-530a-aff6-5a292e2e9897" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,z)=⟨x,\\,{{selection}},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,z)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $12{z}^{2}=y.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,12{z}^{2},\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $12{z}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aed9523-4411-5c93-887e-a453006449b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551477} {:answer/id #uuid "3113557b-876d-5373-80b4-9173b15dee19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551481} {:answer/id #uuid "3665484b-d521-51d5-94f6-ba0ec6688541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551482} {:answer/id #uuid "6857f3fa-5405-5c1c-be67-4bea6e265f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551493} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0b8d93-9d09-5502-b833-56457a7df89d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3665484b-d521-51d5-94f6-ba0ec6688541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551482} :answer-field/id #uuid "efce5fe7-faa0-5338-a592-1c6e07df4370" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fe87002a-bafc-5e6e-87c8-1799a3e17388" :question/problem "$$\nf(z,y)=⟨{{selection}},\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(z,y)$ above parametrizes the parabolic cylinder defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x=7{z}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "For any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(z,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $7{z}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90d9d5b1-0cb8-5f9c-97cb-a379719ac9c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "On any nonempty open region away from the origin, put $r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, $P=-yr^{-p}$, and $Q=xr^{-p}$. Then\n\n$$P_y=-r^{-p}+py^2r^{-p-2},\\qquad Q_x=r^{-p}-px^2r^{-p-2}.$$\n\nHence\n\n$$Q_x-P_y=2r^{-p}-p(x^2+y^2)r^{-p-2}=(2-p)r^{-p}.$$\n\nFor $p\\ne2$ and $r>0$ this is nonzero. A smooth gradient must satisfy $P_y=Q_x$, so the field cannot be conservative on such a region." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90d9d5b1-0cb8-5f9c-97cb-a379719ac9c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "On any nonempty open region away from the origin, put $r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, $P=-yr^{-p}$, and $Q=xr^{-p}$. Then\n\n$$P_y=-r^{-p}+py^2r^{-p-2},\\qquad Q_x=r^{-p}-px^2r^{-p-2}.$$\n\nHence\n\n$$Q_x-P_y=2r^{-p}-p(x^2+y^2)r^{-p-2}=(2-p)r^{-p}.$$\n\nFor $p\\ne2$ and $r>0$ this is nonzero. A smooth gradient must satisfy $P_y=Q_x$, so the field cannot be conservative on such a region." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672278} :answer-field/id #uuid "b731ce8c-86d7-5f82-aaa4-e59d92c96355" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672323}] :question/id #uuid "0565dcad-74ca-5bff-bc5d-ce4a4c286408" :question/problem "Source question: Problem 4, PDF page 5.\n\nShow that the rotation field\n\n$$\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\langle-y,x\\rangle}{(\\sqrt{x^2+y^2})^p}$$\n\nis not conservative for $p\\ne2$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "Nonzero cross-partial difference rules out a potential locally, so no simply-connected assumption is needed for this conclusion. When $p=2$ the difference is zero, but Problem 3(b) explains why global conservativity still depends on the domain."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f42e004-4106-58b8-b22a-4e3372e9ff12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There is not enough data: work depends on the path. The straight segment $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2-t,1+t,-1+4t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$, gives integrand $1+17t$ and work $19/2$. A path changing $x$, then $y$, then $z$ gives $-5/2+1/2+8=6$. These distinct values have the same endpoints." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f42e004-4106-58b8-b22a-4e3372e9ff12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "There is not enough data: work depends on the path. The straight segment $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2-t,1+t,-1+4t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$, gives integrand $1+17t$ and work $19/2$. A path changing $x$, then $y$, then $z$ gives $-5/2+1/2+8=6$. These distinct values have the same endpoints." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672100} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b476a3c-9d55-5ac0-87a6-9808de0d8d81" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672095}] :question/id #uuid "10b1d1b1-c117-5f92-bc32-370541c0d7ae" :question/problem "**(c)** $\\mathbf F=\\langle x+y,y+z,z+x\\rangle$; from $(2,1,-1)$ to $(1,2,3)$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "The components do not satisfy the symmetry required of a gradient; for example $P_y=1$ while $Q_x=0$. The two explicit paths establish that the omitted path matters for these particular endpoints."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85ce1779-ea78-5d27-af4a-89b5cedbfd1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Since $m$ is constant, part (d) gives $d[\\phi(\\mathbf r(t))+(m/2)|\\mathbf v(t)|^2]/dt=0$. Integrating in $t$ yields $\\phi(\\mathbf r(t))+(m/2)|\\mathbf v(t)|^2=C$. The second term is kinetic energy, and the first is potential energy under $\\mathbf F=-\\nabla\\phi$. The constant can be obtained from any initial position and velocity." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85ce1779-ea78-5d27-af4a-89b5cedbfd1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Since $m$ is constant, part (d) gives $d[\\phi(\\mathbf r(t))+(m/2)|\\mathbf v(t)|^2]/dt=0$. Integrating in $t$ yields $\\phi(\\mathbf r(t))+(m/2)|\\mathbf v(t)|^2=C$. The second term is kinetic energy, and the first is potential energy under $\\mathbf F=-\\nabla\\phi$. The constant can be obtained from any initial position and velocity." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672257} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b36cbaf-c3fc-56e1-887d-93eb7cb212a2" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672220}] :question/id #uuid "247ab216-1856-565e-b209-c7484182e3d2" :question/problem "**(e)** Integrate both sides of part (d) with respect to $t$ and show that $\\displaystyle\\phi+\\frac{m|\\mathbf v|^2}{2}=C$, so total kinetic plus potential energy is constant.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "The two integration constants combine into one constant. The conserved quantity has a plus sign: the derivative equation states that a loss of potential energy equals a gain of kinetic energy."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68287a33-4898-579e-b6ce-4d0def049a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "One valid factorization is\n\n$$\nA=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\\\0&1&0\\\\3&0&1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\\\0&0&1\\\\0&1&0\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2&0&0\\\\0&1&0\\\\0&0&1\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}.\n$$\n\nEach factor is elementary: $D$ is obtained from $I_3$ by $R_1\\leftarrow2R_1$, $P$ by $R_2\\leftrightarrow R_3$, and $L$ by $R_3\\leftarrow R_3+3R_1$.\n\nReading right to left, apply $D$ to $I_3$, then $P$, then $L$. The intermediate product is\n\n$$\nPD=\\begin{bmatrix}2&0&0\\\\0&0&1\\\\0&1&0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand adding three times its first row to its third gives\n\n$$\nL(PD)=\\begin{bmatrix}2&0&0\\\\0&0&1\\\\6&1&0\\end{bmatrix}=A.\n$$\n\nOther products are valid if every factor is elementary and the ordered product equals $A$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68287a33-4898-579e-b6ce-4d0def049a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "One valid factorization is\n\n$$\nA=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\\\0&1&0\\\\3&0&1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1&0&0\\\\0&0&1\\\\0&1&0\\end{bmatrix}}_{P}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2&0&0\\\\0&1&0\\\\0&0&1\\end{bmatrix}}_{D}.\n$$\n\nEach factor is elementary: $D$ is obtained from $I_3$ by $R_1\\leftarrow2R_1$, $P$ by $R_2\\leftrightarrow R_3$, and $L$ by $R_3\\leftarrow R_3+3R_1$.\n\nReading right to left, apply $D$ to $I_3$, then $P$, then $L$. The intermediate product is\n\n$$\nPD=\\begin{bmatrix}2&0&0\\\\0&0&1\\\\0&1&0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand adding three times its first row to its third gives\n\n$$\nL(PD)=\\begin{bmatrix}2&0&0\\\\0&0&1\\\\6&1&0\\end{bmatrix}=A.\n$$\n\nOther products are valid if every factor is elementary and the ordered product equals $A$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672215} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b5daf60-78c5-59af-ae07-3dd57957eef2" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672199}] :question/id #uuid "4bb1e95f-ca5b-5a5f-ac14-3c6f7636f6ad" :question/problem "Source question: Assignment item (5), custom problem on the assignment PDF’s only page.\n\nLet\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2&0&0\\\\0&0&1\\\\6&1&0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWrite $A$ as a product of elementary matrices.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "A way to discover the factors is to reduce $A$ to $I_3$: first $R_3\\leftarrow R_3-3R_1$, then $R_2\\leftrightarrow R_3$, then $R_1\\leftarrow\\frac12R_1$. To build $A$ back from $I_3$, undo those operations in reverse chronological order. The resulting factorization is $A=LPD$: the rightmost factor acts first. Check that each factor arises from exactly one row operation on the identity; do not combine several operations into a single factor and call it elementary."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e34b62f-49f2-5d2f-9514-ceb13d7fc467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A potential is $\\phi=x^2+y$, so the work is $\\phi(0,2)-\\phi(2,0)=2-4=-2$. The path is unnecessary because this field is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e34b62f-49f2-5d2f-9514-ceb13d7fc467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A potential is $\\phi=x^2+y$, so the work is $\\phi(0,2)-\\phi(2,0)=2-4=-2$. The path is unnecessary because this field is conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672182} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5e4d656-8daa-55c1-aef4-b324ec88a0ca" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672369}] :question/id #uuid "4d3805f3-82f1-568f-b3c0-c91b66eabfcc" :question/problem "**(a)** $\\mathbf F=\\langle2x,1\\rangle$; from $(2,0)$ to $(0,2)$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "Work equals the final potential minus the initial potential under the mathematical convention $\\mathbf F=\\nabla\\phi$. Preserve the direction from $(2,0)$ to $(0,2)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4afc1fc9-8bed-5fda-9d7a-95df2c467f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "By the dot-product rule, $d(\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf v)/dt=\\mathbf v\\prime\\cdot\\mathbf v+\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf v\\prime=2\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf v\\prime$. The two terms are equal because the dot product is commutative. Since $\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf v=|\\mathbf v|^2$, division by $2$ gives $\\mathbf v\\cdot d\\mathbf v/dt=\\tfrac12\\,d|\\mathbf v|^2/dt$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4afc1fc9-8bed-5fda-9d7a-95df2c467f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "By the dot-product rule, $d(\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf v)/dt=\\mathbf v\\prime\\cdot\\mathbf v+\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf v\\prime=2\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf v\\prime$. The two terms are equal because the dot product is commutative. Since $\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf v=|\\mathbf v|^2$, division by $2$ gives $\\mathbf v\\cdot d\\mathbf v/dt=\\tfrac12\\,d|\\mathbf v|^2/dt$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672174} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d53aa64-799f-5770-8bb0-a4ebc81dc33c" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672151}] :question/id #uuid "58e578c3-0658-5e8d-b1fd-4710cd0d6f6b" :question/problem "**(c)** Show that $$\n\\displaystyle\\frac{d}{dt}(\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf v)=2\\mathbf v\\cdot\\frac{d\\mathbf v}{dt}.\n$$ Conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle\\mathbf v\\cdot\\frac{d\\mathbf v}{dt}=\\frac12\\frac{d}{dt}|\\mathbf v|^2.\n$$\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate both factors. Differentiating only one copy of $\\mathbf v$ loses the factor $2$; no division by speed is needed, so the identity also holds at zero velocity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "493032be-dade-5623-a85d-eb0c063fb53d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "True under the usual continuity assumptions on the component functions. On a product of intervals, define $\\phi(x,y)=\\int_{x_*}^{x}f(u)\\,du+\\int_{y_*}^{y}g(v)\\,dv$. Then $\\phi_x=f(x)$ and $\\phi_y=g(y)$, so $\\nabla\\phi=\\mathbf F$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "493032be-dade-5623-a85d-eb0c063fb53d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "True under the usual continuity assumptions on the component functions. On a product of intervals, define $\\phi(x,y)=\\int_{x_*}^{x}f(u)\\,du+\\int_{y_*}^{y}g(v)\\,dv$. Then $\\phi_x=f(x)$ and $\\phi_y=g(y)$, so $\\nabla\\phi=\\mathbf F$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672169} :answer-field/id #uuid "20014a4b-ab56-5659-aa45-9b9013275ab3" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672101}] :question/id #uuid "6c441682-01a2-55c4-b638-466c6f36550b" :question/problem "**(b)** $\\mathbf F=\\langle f(x),g(y)\\rangle$ is conservative.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "Each component depends only on its matching coordinate, so its antiderivative contributes only to the corresponding partial derivative. Do not merely cite equality of cross-partials without checking the working domain; here the explicit potential supplies the proof."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1b9e1d5-e76e-573c-b67f-97f3f4637728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Newton’s second law is $\\mathbf F=m\\mathbf a$, and acceleration is $\\mathbf a=d\\mathbf v/dt$. Substituting the stated physics convention $\\mathbf F=-\\nabla\\phi$ yields $-\\nabla\\phi=m\\,d\\mathbf v/dt$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1b9e1d5-e76e-573c-b67f-97f3f4637728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Newton’s second law is $\\mathbf F=m\\mathbf a$, and acceleration is $\\mathbf a=d\\mathbf v/dt$. Substituting the stated physics convention $\\mathbf F=-\\nabla\\phi$ yields $-\\nabla\\phi=m\\,d\\mathbf v/dt$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672388} :answer-field/id #uuid "4312ad06-ce63-51d3-b3d9-cf07c806d206" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672158}] :question/id #uuid "74b01add-4b8f-5e83-8135-6325fb74bcc4" :question/problem "**(b)** Show that Newton’s law of motion can be written as $\\displaystyle-\\nabla\\phi=m\\frac{d\\mathbf v}{dt}$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "The minus sign comes from the source’s definition of potential energy. It must be retained when substituting into Newton’s law; it is not the mathematical convention used in earlier potential-function problems."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28db0224-412b-56c9-bd93-6de66ee2f02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Dot the equation in part (b) with $\\mathbf r\\prime=\\mathbf v$: $-\\nabla\\phi\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime=m(d\\mathbf v/dt)\\cdot\\mathbf v=m\\mathbf v\\cdot(d\\mathbf v/dt)$. Part (a) identifies the left side as $-d\\phi/dt$, and part (c) identifies the right side as $(m/2)d|\\mathbf v|^2/dt$. Thus $-d\\phi/dt=(m/2)d|\\mathbf v|^2/dt$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28db0224-412b-56c9-bd93-6de66ee2f02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Dot the equation in part (b) with $\\mathbf r\\prime=\\mathbf v$: $-\\nabla\\phi\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime=m(d\\mathbf v/dt)\\cdot\\mathbf v=m\\mathbf v\\cdot(d\\mathbf v/dt)$. Part (a) identifies the left side as $-d\\phi/dt$, and part (c) identifies the right side as $(m/2)d|\\mathbf v|^2/dt$. Thus $-d\\phi/dt=(m/2)d|\\mathbf v|^2/dt$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672122} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4652eb3-d8a4-5c17-b3b8-37cc631ad025" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672319}] :question/id #uuid "74dd772d-5164-5c5b-b7c4-d12977f4634a" :question/problem "**(d)** Use part (b) to show that $$\n\\displaystyle-\\nabla\\phi\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime=m\\mathbf v\\cdot\\frac{d\\mathbf v}{dt}.\n$$ Conclude from parts (a) and (c) that $$\n\\displaystyle-\\frac{d\\phi}{dt}=\\frac m2\\frac{d}{dt}|\\mathbf v|^2.\n$$\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "This step combines the chain rule, Newton’s second law, and the dot-product identity. The negative potential-energy rate equals the positive kinetic-energy rate."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "a0b2ac5f-b043-5418-96ff-29d8f463215c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672298}] :question/id #uuid "7d6e627f-e65e-527b-8b71-71d075a6c30f" :question/problem "Source question: Canvas Question 2 (question_59459083).\n\nWhat did you find most helpful for you in studying for the MiniExam 1? Choose all that apply.\n\nc1. In-class activities\n\nc2. Written Homeworks\n\nc3. Re-reading the book\n\nc4. Videos created by our instructors\n\nc5. Videos created external to the course\n\nc6. Studying with other people\n\nc7. Other\n\nList all applicable choices: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "This is a personal reflection. Select every option that describes what helped you prepare for MiniExam 1. No selection or combination is prescribed as correct.\n\n**c1:** Select this if it describes your own experience; this survey option has no objective correctness.\n\n**c2:** Select this if it describes your own experience; this survey option has no objective correctness.\n\n**c3:** Select this if it describes your own experience; this survey option has no objective correctness.\n\n**c4:** Select this if it describes your own experience; this survey option has no objective correctness.\n\n**c5:** Select this if it describes your own experience; this survey option has no objective correctness.\n\n**c6:** Select this if it describes your own experience; this survey option has no objective correctness.\n\n**c7:** Select this if it describes your own experience; this survey option has no objective correctness."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6164d36e-d283-5f0c-becd-f938418b6468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "False. Parametrize $C$ by $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle1+2\\cos t,2\\sin t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. Then $\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime=4+2\\cos t$, so the circulation is $8\\pi$. Equivalently, Green’s theorem gives $\\iint_D(1-(-1))\\,dA=2(4\\pi)=8\\pi$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6164d36e-d283-5f0c-becd-f938418b6468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "False. Parametrize $C$ by $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle1+2\\cos t,2\\sin t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. Then $\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r)\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime=4+2\\cos t$, so the circulation is $8\\pi$. Equivalently, Green’s theorem gives $\\iint_D(1-(-1))\\,dA=2(4\\pi)=8\\pi$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672209} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ca88fb-458a-59a7-a457-3e41f21aa7c7" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672119}] :question/id #uuid "9612be23-c4fa-5bf7-9a29-bdbe6b83cc38" :question/problem "**(d)** $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=0\n$$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x\\rangle$ and the counterclockwise circle of radius $2$ centered at $(1,0)$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "A closed path alone does not make a line integral zero. The sign is positive for the stated counterclockwise orientation. The center shift contributes a cosine term whose integral over a full period is zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77e30db5-96dc-5213-a763-31994eb5e81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Put $r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}>0$. A potential is\n\n$$\\phi(x,y)=\\begin{cases}\\dfrac{r^{2-p}}{2-p}+C,&p\\ne2,\\\\ \\ln r+C,&p=2.\\end{cases}$$\n\nFor $p\\ne2$, $\\partial_x\\phi=r^{1-p}(x/r)=xr^{-p}$, and similarly $\\partial_y\\phi=yr^{-p}$. For $p=2$, $\\nabla\\ln r=\\langle x/r^2,y/r^2\\rangle$. Both potentials are single-valued and smooth on the entire punctured plane, so restricting them to $R$ proves the claim without a simply-connected assumption." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77e30db5-96dc-5213-a763-31994eb5e81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Put $r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}>0$. A potential is\n\n$$\\phi(x,y)=\\begin{cases}\\dfrac{r^{2-p}}{2-p}+C,&p\\ne2,\\\\ \\ln r+C,&p=2.\\end{cases}$$\n\nFor $p\\ne2$, $\\partial_x\\phi=r^{1-p}(x/r)=xr^{-p}$, and similarly $\\partial_y\\phi=yr^{-p}$. For $p=2$, $\\nabla\\ln r=\\langle x/r^2,y/r^2\\rangle$. Both potentials are single-valued and smooth on the entire punctured plane, so restricting them to $R$ proves the claim without a simply-connected assumption." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672239} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bfd88ea-8539-588d-965f-3eb6d2a3f446" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672294}] :question/id #uuid "b7613863-ba6f-5d12-838c-52f900707ebf" :question/problem "**(a)** Show that the radial field\n\n$$\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\mathbf r}{|\\mathbf r|^p}=\\frac{\\langle x,y\\rangle}{(\\sqrt{x^2+y^2})^p}$$\n\nis conservative on $R$. Find a potential for $p\\ne2$, and then separately for $p=2$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "The exceptional case $p=2$ is logarithmic. Writing $\\ln r=\\tfrac12\\ln(x^2+y^2)$ is equivalent. Differentiate the proposed potential to verify both coordinates; the excluded origin keeps all real powers well-defined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "243d9b50-fd80-5629-a201-d39edaa49f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "True. For a constant field $\\mathbf F=\\langle a,b\\rangle$, the function $\\phi(x,y)=ax+by$ satisfies $\\nabla\\phi=\\langle a,b\\rangle$. In three dimensions use $\\phi=ax+by+cz$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672110}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "243d9b50-fd80-5629-a201-d39edaa49f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "True. For a constant field $\\mathbf F=\\langle a,b\\rangle$, the function $\\phi(x,y)=ax+by$ satisfies $\\nabla\\phi=\\langle a,b\\rangle$. In three dimensions use $\\phi=ax+by+cz$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672110} :answer-field/id #uuid "29f5ef1e-3252-5e74-8d83-e19289f01bba" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672126}] :question/id #uuid "c49e8f4b-bbc7-56d8-a44d-3bdf947c8693" :question/problem "**(a)** Constant vector fields are conservative.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "A potential proves the claim directly. Restricting this globally defined potential to any region preserves the equality $\\nabla\\phi=\\mathbf F$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672350 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156f8824-8b5b-5523-b19f-b90e8d1de836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A potential is $\\phi=(x^2+y^2+z^2)/2$. Thus $W=(1+4+9)/2-(4+1+1)/2=7-3=4$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "156f8824-8b5b-5523-b19f-b90e8d1de836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "A potential is $\\phi=(x^2+y^2+z^2)/2$. Thus $W=(1+4+9)/2-(4+1+1)/2=7-3=4$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672087} :answer-field/id #uuid "977fb05d-c66d-5b88-90c1-8c577328eb1c" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672289}] :question/id #uuid "c83c10cf-0d2e-5146-8514-99d6a82eaf1c" :question/problem "**(b)** $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$; from $(2,1,-1)$ to $(1,2,3)$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of half the squared distance from the origin is $\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$. Evaluate that potential at the endpoints; no path is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7827fdd8-24b8-556b-8497-0a65c01f9a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Write $\\phi=\\phi(x(t),y(t),z(t))$. The chain rule gives $d\\phi/dt=\\phi_x x\\prime+\\phi_y y\\prime+\\phi_z z\\prime=\\langle\\phi_x,\\phi_y,\\phi_z\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x\\prime,y\\prime,z\\prime\\rangle=\\nabla\\phi\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime$. All spatial derivatives are evaluated at $\\mathbf r(t)$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7827fdd8-24b8-556b-8497-0a65c01f9a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Write $\\phi=\\phi(x(t),y(t),z(t))$. The chain rule gives $d\\phi/dt=\\phi_x x\\prime+\\phi_y y\\prime+\\phi_z z\\prime=\\langle\\phi_x,\\phi_y,\\phi_z\\rangle\\cdot\\langle x\\prime,y\\prime,z\\prime\\rangle=\\nabla\\phi\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime$. All spatial derivatives are evaluated at $\\mathbf r(t)$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672240} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a181819-2de3-5e49-80d6-51a7ac7822c0" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672143}] :question/id #uuid "c8f5e6fd-4e8e-51ce-8575-c536966cb869" :question/problem "**(a)** Use the chain rule to show that $\\displaystyle\\frac{d\\phi}{dt}=\\nabla\\phi\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "The three coordinate paths are the three dependencies of $\\phi$ on $t$. Include all three terms and evaluate the gradient along the motion."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c909d698-4d34-5d8a-b569-33ac833d3361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "True. A potential is $\\phi=(x^2-y^2)/2$. Since $C$ is closed, the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r=\\phi(\\text{end})-\\phi(\\text{start})=0$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c909d698-4d34-5d8a-b569-33ac833d3361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "True. A potential is $\\phi=(x^2-y^2)/2$. Since $C$ is closed, the fundamental theorem for line integrals gives $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r=\\phi(\\text{end})-\\phi(\\text{start})=0$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672353} :answer-field/id #uuid "b153f010-c786-5444-940f-9249caf7cc4d" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672315}] :question/id #uuid "d7fb55bf-6bac-58f5-84c1-9372d13167e6" :question/problem "**(c)** $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=0\n$$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,-y\\rangle$ and the counterclockwise circle of radius $2$ centered at $(1,0)$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "Check $\\nabla\\phi=\\langle x,-y\\rangle$ and note that the polynomial potential is defined everywhere, including on the circle. A closed-curve integral of a globally defined gradient is zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2c0e532-ba22-55ca-aea6-1f8868df6c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "For $x\\ne0$, differentiate to get $y\\prime=2x/3-c/x^2$. Then $xy\\prime+y=2x^2/3-c/x+x^2/3+c/x=x^2$. Thus it solves the equation on each interval avoiding zero. The source expression is part of the task, not an answer to hide." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2c0e532-ba22-55ca-aea6-1f8868df6c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "For $x\\ne0$, differentiate to get $y\\prime=2x/3-c/x^2$. Then $xy\\prime+y=2x^2/3-c/x+x^2/3+c/x=x^2$. Thus it solves the equation on each interval avoiding zero. The source expression is part of the task, not an answer to hide." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672392} :answer-field/id #uuid "209cf08d-315a-5928-b1bb-6c43a6428257" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672102}] :question/id #uuid "da114faf-bf62-52a8-8204-3403da36b8cc" :question/problem "Source question: Problem 2, question PDF page 2.\n\nVerify that $y=x^2/3+c/x$ solves $xy'+y=x^2$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "For $x\\ne0$, differentiate to get $y\\prime=2x/3-c/x^2$. Then $xy\\prime+y=2x^2/3-c/x+x^2/3+c/x=x^2$. Thus it solves the equation on each interval avoiding zero. The source expression is part of the task, not an answer to hide."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea1b17ed-64ca-55b2-934b-ce9654281bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No, on the displayed plane region. Write the field as $\\mathbf F=\\langle P(y),Q(y)\\rangle$. Its horizontal component changes with height: it points left below the axis and right above it. Take a rectangle spanning those two heights. The vertical-edge work cancels because the field is independent of $x$, while the horizontal-edge work is the width times $P(y_{\\rm bottom})-P(y_{\\rm top})$, which is nonzero. A conservative field would have zero work around every closed rectangle." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea1b17ed-64ca-55b2-934b-ce9654281bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No, on the displayed plane region. Write the field as $\\mathbf F=\\langle P(y),Q(y)\\rangle$. Its horizontal component changes with height: it points left below the axis and right above it. Take a rectangle spanning those two heights. The vertical-edge work cancels because the field is independent of $x$, while the horizontal-edge work is the width times $P(y_{\\rm bottom})-P(y_{\\rm top})$, which is nonzero. A conservative field would have zero work around every closed rectangle." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672398} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc558e44-1051-515d-b9ae-3057077a9f37" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672332}] :question/id #uuid "e2aaacb3-a89d-57b5-944e-1f0ad09b7e12" :question/problem "Source question: Problem 1, PDF page 2.\n\nThe following two-dimensional field depends only on $y$. Is it conservative? Explain.\n\n![Source vector field whose arrows vary with height](/api/asset?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FWHW-4%2Fhomework-4-vector-field.png)\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "The vertical dependence alone does not establish conservativity. For a smooth field of this form, the condition is $P\\prime(y)=Q_x=0$, so the horizontal component must be constant. Use only the directions shown; no numerical formula or arrow magnitude is supplied by the figure."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186672424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31d4c26f-7fe8-50c0-b675-0c7d94f486db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The assertion is false for an arbitrary region excluding the origin. On $R=\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{(0,0)\\}$, the counterclockwise unit circle gives\n\n$$\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^{2\\pi}1\\,dt=2\\pi\\ne0.$$\n\nConsequently there is no globally defined potential on that region. The field is locally a gradient: on $x>0$, one potential is $\\phi(x,y)=\\arctan(y/x)$, whose gradient is $\\langle-y/(x^2+y^2),x/(x^2+y^2)\\rangle$. More generally, an angle branch supplies a potential on a suitable branch domain, and a simply-connected open region avoiding the origin admits a potential. Merely excluding the origin does not supply that extra domain condition." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31d4c26f-7fe8-50c0-b675-0c7d94f486db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The assertion is false for an arbitrary region excluding the origin. On $R=\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{(0,0)\\}$, the counterclockwise unit circle gives\n\n$$\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^{2\\pi}1\\,dt=2\\pi\\ne0.$$\n\nConsequently there is no globally defined potential on that region. The field is locally a gradient: on $x>0$, one potential is $\\phi(x,y)=\\arctan(y/x)$, whose gradient is $\\langle-y/(x^2+y^2),x/(x^2+y^2)\\rangle$. More generally, an angle branch supplies a potential on a suitable branch domain, and a simply-connected open region avoiding the origin admits a potential. Merely excluding the origin does not supply that extra domain condition." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672135} :answer-field/id #uuid "e30c2349-ffe3-5b7a-a76d-c9a3d2f7831b" :answer-field/key "response" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186672393}] :question/id #uuid "fa7a3e96-b220-5548-b443-22dcf61c1bca" :question/problem "**(b)** The source asks: Show that the rotation field\n\n$$\\mathbf F=\\frac{\\langle-y,x\\rangle}{x^2+y^2}$$\n\nis conservative on $R$. Find a potential function.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" :question/worked-solution "This is a source-statement limitation, not an unreadable question. Equality of cross-partials is only a local condition on the punctured plane; a hole can prevent a global potential. Preserve the original request, then explain the counterexample and the additional domain condition rather than writing a false proof."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c2c23da-b34b-5ab9-b132-1f49740e6c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)}{5 \\cdot C + 2 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673241} {:answer/id #uuid "53da8360-1ce2-54da-adcb-fc0a51f23a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{9 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)^{3}}{9 \\cdot C - 2 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)^{\\frac{9}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673246} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdabab2-bb4f-5c10-8bcd-1244abaa3cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)^{2}}{7 \\cdot C - 6 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)^{\\frac{7}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673254} {:answer/id #uuid "902ea5b5-35a5-540e-b609-df04938119bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{9 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)^{3}}{9 \\cdot C + 2 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)^{\\frac{9}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673260} {:answer/id #uuid "f4645217-8b7e-504b-a22d-f5a0e6956ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)^{2}}{7 \\cdot C + 4 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)^{\\frac{7}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c2c23da-b34b-5ab9-b132-1f49740e6c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)}{5 \\cdot C + 2 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673241} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fddbb82-af92-5514-a0b4-32c7712b6ca3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5801f052-b0ad-5933-bf26-c141fa478197" :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}- \\frac{y}{\\left(x+3\\right)}=- \\sqrt{\\left(x+3\\right)} \\cdot y^{2}.\n$$\n\nWork on an interval where $\\left(x+3\\right)>0$. Choose constants and intervals on which the displayed expressions are real and finite.\n\n{{selection}}" :question/worked-solution "This is a Bernoulli equation with\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}p(x)&=- \\frac{1}{\\left(x+3\\right)}\\\\ q(x)&=- \\sqrt{\\left(x+3\\right)}\\\\ n&=2.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nDivide by $y^{2}$ and set $z=y^{-1}$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- \\frac{1}{y^{2}}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$$\n\nHence the linear equation in standard form is\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\left(\\frac{1}{\\left(x+3\\right)}\\right)z=\\sqrt{\\left(x+3\\right)}.$$\n\nAn integrating factor is\n\n$$I(x)=e^{\\int \\frac{1}{\\left(x+3\\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=\\left(x+3\\right).$$\n\nMultiplying through and using the product rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\left(x+3\\right)\\,z\\right)=\\left(x+3\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}.$$\n\nIntegrating with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\\left(x+3\\right)z=\\frac{2 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{5}+C.$$\n\nThus\n\n$$z=\\frac{C}{\\left(x+3\\right)} + \\frac{2 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{5}.$$\n\nFinally, substitute $z=y^{-1}$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$y=\\frac{5 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)}{5 \\cdot C + 2 \\cdot \\left(x+3\\right)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}.$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary real constant; exclude singularities and restrict to the stated real intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27053826-774a-5778-a46c-55339c8259ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\left(C \\cdot e^{\\frac{x}{2}} + 3\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673239} {:answer/id #uuid "63a0a3f6-7a6e-53d0-870b-cb1f0d8e828e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\left(C \\cdot e^{\\frac{3 \\cdot x}{2}} + \\frac{1}{3}\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673251} {:answer/id #uuid "7920eb08-55a3-5637-8b43-d9eaab143d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\left(C \\cdot e^{\\frac{x}{2}} - 2\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673256} {:answer/id #uuid "86cd5af9-33cf-57f3-a021-5cd4130bfe9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\left(C \\cdot e^{\\frac{x}{2}} - 1\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673258} {:answer/id #uuid "c6af010a-d598-520a-bb6d-0ecb2c8813c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\left(C \\cdot e^{\\frac{3 \\cdot x}{2}} + \\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7920eb08-55a3-5637-8b43-d9eaab143d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\left(C \\cdot e^{\\frac{x}{2}} - 2\\right)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673256} :answer-field/id #uuid "70ed373a-f44b-519c-bee2-72da579302ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "63e30119-1a5b-5cbc-bb91-0a68fadb5bb2" :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}- y=2 \\cdot \\sqrt{y}.\n$$ Take $y>0$. For even powers and powers of $2/3$ in the choices, retain only intervals where the base is positive. Choose constants and intervals on which the displayed expressions are real and finite.\n\n{{selection}}" :question/worked-solution "This is a Bernoulli equation with\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}p(x)&=-1\\\\ q(x)&=2\\\\ n&=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nDivide by $y^{\\frac{1}{2}}$ and set $z=y^{\\frac{1}{2}}$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2 \\cdot \\sqrt{y}}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$$\n\nHence the linear equation in standard form is\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)z=1.$$\n\nAn integrating factor is\n\n$$I(x)=e^{\\int - \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}.$$\n\nMultiplying through and using the product rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}\\,z\\right)=e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}.$$\n\nIntegrating with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}z=- 2 \\cdot e^{- \\frac{x}{2}}+C.$$\n\nThus\n\n$$z=C \\cdot e^{\\frac{x}{2}} - 2.$$\n\nFinally, substitute $z=y^{\\frac{1}{2}}$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$y=\\left(C \\cdot e^{\\frac{x}{2}} - 2\\right)^{2}.$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary real constant; exclude singularities and restrict to the stated real intervals. The displayed expression for $z$ must be positive because $z=y^{\\frac{1}{2}}>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ed69f91-4032-5c77-a624-ac7493dade1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\pm\\sqrt{- \\frac{4}{- 4 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{4 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right)} + 4 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right) + 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673244} {:answer/id #uuid "5109e732-2705-5b2d-a856-456b9770101f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{6}{6 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{6 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right)} + 6 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right) + 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673245} {:answer/id #uuid "8807cf27-732b-520d-95fb-8fd18cb5b26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{C \\cdot e^{2 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right)} + 2 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right) + 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673259} {:answer/id #uuid "906b9ddc-81e6-511c-bf11-8c2edb395b02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\pm\\sqrt{- \\frac{1}{- C \\cdot e^{2 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right)} + 2 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right) + 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673261} {:answer/id #uuid "b3a8cc80-e0bf-54ce-9bf9-ff81ebc9261f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{8 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{4 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right)} + 12 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right) + 3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5109e732-2705-5b2d-a856-456b9770101f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{6}{6 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{6 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right)} + 6 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right) + 1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673245} :answer-field/id #uuid "effadeda-4df7-5f84-bf7c-c73637ff4c81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673269}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b11bcb99-57dd-5f41-b31d-d4bfd1f23316" :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+3 \\cdot y=3 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right) \\cdot y^{3}.\n$$\n\nWork on an interval where $\\left(x-3\\right)>0$. Choose constants and intervals on which the displayed expressions are real and finite.\n\n{{selection}}" :question/worked-solution "This is a Bernoulli equation with\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}p(x)&=3\\\\ q(x)&=3 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right)\\\\ n&=3.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nDivide by $y^{3}$ and set $z=y^{-2}$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- \\frac{2}{y^{3}}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$$\n\nHence the linear equation in standard form is\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\left(-6\\right)z=- 6 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right).$$\n\nAn integrating factor is\n\n$$I(x)=e^{\\int -6\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{- 6 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right)}.$$\n\nMultiplying through and using the product rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(e^{- 6 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right)}\\,z\\right)=- 6 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right) \\cdot e^{- 6 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right)}.$$\n\nIntegrating with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$e^{- 6 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right)}z=\\left(\\left(x-3\\right) + \\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\cdot e^{- 6 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right)}+C.$$\n\nThus\n\n$$z=C \\cdot e^{6 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right)} + \\left(x-3\\right) + \\frac{1}{6}.$$\n\nFinally, substitute $z=y^{-2}$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$y=\\pm\\sqrt{\\frac{6}{6 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{6 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right)} + 6 \\cdot \\left(x-3\\right) + 1}}.$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary real constant; exclude singularities and restrict to the stated real intervals. Both signs are retained wherever the radicand is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d56d7e9-d563-5355-967b-957ca3f2b2b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[5]{C \\cdot e^{\\frac{10 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{3}}{3}} - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673243} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8c9224-7464-5b3d-9481-901d66114377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[5]{C \\cdot e^{\\frac{10 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{3}}{3}} + \\frac{1}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673253} {:answer/id #uuid "af482571-f59f-510d-8968-932276b6af54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[5]{C \\cdot e^{\\frac{5 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{3}}{3}} + 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673262} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd26068-c8b0-5974-9881-80cb506bef69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[5]{C \\cdot e^{\\frac{5 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{3}}{3}} - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673267} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9a6a0c-e58b-5f2a-b4e1-e7a057e2e002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[5]{C \\cdot e^{\\frac{5 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{3}}{3}} - 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af482571-f59f-510d-8968-932276b6af54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt[5]{C \\cdot e^{\\frac{5 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{3}}{3}} + 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673262} :answer-field/id #uuid "46f8b52d-7488-5cfe-96a2-6f6270c23b92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673242}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c98ebde1-9cae-55b5-a3ae-6eb0a074c80f" :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}- \\left(x+2\\right)^{2} \\cdot y=- \\frac{3 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{2}}{y^{4}}.\n$$\n\nWork on an interval where $\\left(x+2\\right)>0$. Choose constants and intervals on which the displayed expressions are real and finite.\n\n{{selection}}" :question/worked-solution "This is a Bernoulli equation with\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}p(x)&=- \\left(x+2\\right)^{2}\\\\ q(x)&=- 3 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{2}\\\\ n&=-4.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nDivide by $y^{-4}$ and set $z=y^{5}$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}=5 \\cdot y^{4}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$$\n\nHence the linear equation in standard form is\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\left(- 5 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{2}\\right)z=- 15 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{2}.$$\n\nAn integrating factor is\n\n$$I(x)=e^{\\int - 5 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{- \\frac{5 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{3}}{3}}.$$\n\nMultiplying through and using the product rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(e^{- \\frac{5 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{3}}{3}}\\,z\\right)=- 15 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{2} \\cdot e^{- \\frac{5 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{3}}{3}}.$$\n\nIntegrating with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$e^{- \\frac{5 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{3}}{3}}z=3 \\cdot e^{- \\frac{5 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{3}}{3}}+C.$$\n\nThus\n\n$$z=C \\cdot e^{\\frac{5 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{3}}{3}} + 3.$$\n\nFinally, substitute $z=y^{5}$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$y=\\sqrt[5]{C \\cdot e^{\\frac{5 \\cdot \\left(x+2\\right)^{3}}{3}} + 3}.$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary real constant; exclude singularities and restrict to the stated real intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05a764a0-688a-5f0e-a394-b1e79f141e79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{2 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{\\left(x+5\\right)^{2}} - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673237} {:answer/id #uuid "0a2ba7b5-54ce-5538-be65-a8f53b901840" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{C \\cdot e^{\\frac{3 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}{2}} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673238} {:answer/id #uuid "5a38801e-0944-513c-b15f-776143dfd915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{3 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{\\frac{3 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}{2}} + 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673248} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2255c5-232a-537b-a15b-31fad33d524c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{C \\cdot e^{\\frac{\\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}{2}} - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673249} {:answer/id #uuid "857e71a6-9966-5a31-aae6-7d316fcf2323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{2 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{\\left(x+5\\right)^{2}} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "857e71a6-9966-5a31-aae6-7d316fcf2323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{2 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{\\left(x+5\\right)^{2}} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673257} :answer-field/id #uuid "37fce50a-669c-5904-b80e-2fbfd3c0cec3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f42d077c-e368-53a5-991d-e999c0fb2c03" :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+2 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right) \\cdot y=\\left(x+5\\right) \\cdot y^{2}.\n$$\n\nWork on an interval where $\\left(x+5\\right)>0$. Choose constants and intervals on which the displayed expressions are real and finite.\n\n{{selection}}" :question/worked-solution "This is a Bernoulli equation with\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}p(x)&=2 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)\\\\ q(x)&=\\left(x+5\\right)\\\\ n&=2.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nDivide by $y^{2}$ and set $z=y^{-1}$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- \\frac{1}{y^{2}}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$$\n\nHence the linear equation in standard form is\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\left(- 2 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)\\right)z=- \\left(x+5\\right).$$\n\nAn integrating factor is\n\n$$I(x)=e^{\\int - 2 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{- \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}.$$\n\nMultiplying through and using the product rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(e^{- \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}\\,z\\right)=- \\left(x+5\\right) \\cdot e^{- \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}.$$\n\nIntegrating with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$e^{- \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}z=\\frac{e^{- \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}}{2}+C.$$\n\nThus\n\n$$z=C \\cdot e^{\\left(x+5\\right)^{2}} + \\frac{1}{2}.$$\n\nFinally, substitute $z=y^{-1}$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$y=\\frac{2}{2 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{\\left(x+5\\right)^{2}} + 1}.$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary real constant; exclude singularities and restrict to the stated real intervals."}]}
